## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of AWS-native DevOps practices applied to SAP ABAP environments, requiring knowledge of both AWS supply chain security principles and SAP transport management realities.

## Key Concepts
- AWS CodePipeline, CodeBuild, CodeArtifact integration
- SAP ABAP/NetWeaver transport and change management
- IAM least privilege and environment segregation
- Cryptographic signing with AWS Signer and KMS
- Deployment governance and automated rollback strategies

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "codebuild:StartBuild",
        "codebuild:BatchGetBuilds"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:codebuild:*:*:project/abap-build-*"
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "codeartifact:PublishPackageVersion",
        "codeartifact:ReadFromRepository"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:codeartifact:*:*:repository/abap-addons/*"
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "signer:StartSigningJob",
        "signer:GetSigningProfile"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:signer:*:*:signing-profile/abap-signing-*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design scalable, auditable cross-account access that bridges SAP authorizations with AWS IAM, while using JIT and MFA in a multi-region, multi-account setup. Tests integration points across IAM, SCPs, SSO, auditing, and basic IaC.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account trust and least privilege
- SCPs and IAM role mappings to SAP authorizations
- Just‑In‑Time elevation with AWS SSO and MFA
- Auditing: CloudTrail, Config, Central logging
- Minimal IaC for trust role and VPC endpoint service

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal Terraform-like snippet for cross-account trust role (pseudo)
provider "aws" { alias = "shared" }
resource "aws_iam_role" "sap_operator_trust" {
  name = "sap-operator-cross-account"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow",
        Principal = { AWS = ["arn:aws:iam::111111111111:root"] },
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole",
        Condition = { StringEquals = { "sts:ExternalId" = "sap-access" } }
      }
    ]
  })
}

// Per‑BU session policy (inline for example)
resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "sap_operator_policy" {
  name = "sap-operator-permission"
  role = aws_iam_role.sap_operator_trust.name
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [
      { Effect = "Allow", Action = ["ec2:Describe*"], Resource = "*" },
      { Effect = "Allow", Action = ["sap:*"], Resource = "*" }
    ]
  })
}

// VPC Endpoint Service placeholder
resource "aws_vpc_endpoint_service" "sap_endpoints" {
  acceptance_required = true
  network_load_balancer_arns = [aws_lb.sap_nlb.arn]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the mapping between SAP authorizations and AWS roles at scale?
- How would you handle key rotation and revocation in this model?

